messages:
sarah: quick follow-up to the retries/pagination RFC — we need concrete SDK patterns for Python/TS/Go that behave the same for users. primary constraints: different language idioms (sync/async/goroutines), transports (grpc streaming vs http chunking), and the need for safe retries on paginated endpoints. looking for tradeoffs and an implementation sketch.

miguel: +1. two big axes: (1) ergonomics per language idiom (sync iterator vs async iterator vs Next(ctx)) and (2) retry semantics (per-request attempt vs resume-from-cursor). for streaming grpc we can't re-run mid-stream without server support for resume cursors. i think we should default to cursor-based pagination on server side for any list endpoints we expect to retry.

anjali: agree on cursor-first. also we should make retry policy pluggable at client creation time, not per-call, with sensible defaults. defaults: idempotency-aware (lists/get = retryable), 5 max attempts, exp backoff with jitter, overall deadline enforced by context/deadline. include opt-in for "retry_on_nonidempotent" for advanced users.

kevin: a concern: grpc server streaming is commonly used for low-latency paging where server streams each item. if we require cursor-aware resumability we need an efficient way to emit a resume token in-stream (e.g., periodically emit last-seen-cursor in envelope). otherwise retries would either restart from first page or be blocked. that has cost. are we okay with requiring server changes?

sarah: yeah — for rollout we can do: new paginated endpoints must return a resume cursor in the response footer OR support an explicit ListPage(cursor) param. older endpoints keep best-effort semantics (client-level retry only for full-page failures). sample UX sketches:

```go
// Go iterator
it := client.ListWidgets(ctx, opts)
for it.Next(ctx) {
  w := it.Item()
  // handle
}
if err := it.Err(); err != nil {
  // err may be final (non-retriable) or retried-away
}
```

```ts
// TS async iterator
for await (const page of client.listWidgets({pageSize: 100})) {
  for (const item of page.items) {
    // handle
  }
}
```

python sketch (sync + async):
```py
for page in client.list_widgets(page_size=100):
    for item in page.items:
        ...
# async
async for page in client.list_widgets_async(page_size=100):
    ...
```

miguel: those shapes look good. important: surface cancellation consistently. Go uses ctx; Python should honor timeout / cancel via passed-in CancellationToken or context manager; TS should accept an AbortSignal. doc this explicitly. also add headers: x-retry-attempt and x-resume-cursor for telemetry/debug.

anjali: about backoff granularity: we should backoff per-attempt (per failing RPC) not per item. base=100ms, multiplier=2, jitter=full, max_attempts=5, max_backoff=10s — these are reasonable defaults. but expose as config with server-side overrides for very latency-sensitive customers. also, telemetry: emit metric retry_attempts_total and labels for operation/paginated. include traceparent propagation and a retry.counter span attribute.

kevin: idempotency detection — can we auto-detect? lists and gets are easy. "generate"/mutating APIs are not. i think require explicit flag for mutating calls if user wants retries, and if user sets that the SDK should warn about potential duplication. maybe provide idempotency_key header for server use.

sarah: agreed. default policy: lists/paginators = retryable by default; generate/chat = non-retriable by default. allow explicit consent: retry_policy.allow_non_idempotent = true and optional idempotency_key param. add examples in README. :thumbsup:

miguel: transport fallback needs clarity. if gRPC fails with Unavailable, prefer automatic retry at transport layer. if the client configured http fallback, attempt http/1.1 json with same retry policy but note streaming differences — convert stream to page-at-a-time requests. propose a transport adapter interface:

- Transport.openStream(req) -> StreamHandle (supports cancel/close, resume if server supports)
- Transport.unary(req) -> Response

Then SDK surface uses the adapter contract and language-specific impls for gRPC/http. keeps retry logic above transport adapter.

anjali: that adapter looks good. actionable: write an RFC v2 that includes the adapter, default retry config, iterator shapes per language, and migration guide for endpoints without resume tokens. i'll own the doc.

ci-bot: deploy-notes: created rfc/retries-pagination-v2.md in docs repo, assigned to anjali, reviewers: sarah,miguel,kevin.

sarah: sweet. action items: (1) anjali to draft RFC and examples (2) miguel to prototype transport adapter in go/ts (3) kevin to audit existing paginated endpoints and mark which need resume-token work (4) i'll update client idiom notes for python/ts/go. we'll sync next week. thanks all.
